1
00:00:03,475 --> 00:00:05,420
Hello, I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

2
00:00:05,421 --> 00:00:13,420
This time, before starting character concept work, let's talk about how to gather and use references.

3
00:00:13,580 --> 00:00:20,421
I'll let you know upfront that this lecture contains more of my subjective thoughts than any other.

4
00:00:23,805 --> 00:00:27,160
Last time, we decided what to draw.

5
00:00:27,161 --> 00:00:29,160
Then we need to find references.

6
00:00:29,694 --> 00:00:33,560
References differ depending on what I need.

7
00:00:33,640 --> 00:00:46,360
Back when I was a job seeker or a newbie, I focused on drawing realistically, and even when drawing character concepts, I used to only look for face photos, hand photos, body photos, and such.

8
00:00:46,685 --> 00:00:49,560
Looking back now, that was a very wrong idea.

9
00:00:49,561 --> 00:00:53,560
A concept artist's main goal isn't to draw well like a photo.

10
00:00:53,865 --> 00:00:56,260
It's to design well.

11
00:00:56,261 --> 00:01:00,740
Depicting something realistically is only to maximize communication.

12
00:01:00,741 --> 00:01:11,220
But even while using references this wrongly, there's something I kept, and still keep to this day: using photos as much as possible for reference.

13
00:01:11,221 --> 00:01:18,100
A drawing is a reconstruction of someone's story, and it contains that person's own original design or thoughts.

14
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:22,141
It's someone else's conclusion, and it can't be mine.

15
00:01:22,334 --> 00:01:30,040
In realistic-style drawing, if I have something I want to say through the picture, there's no need to follow an answer composed by someone else.

16
00:01:30,041 --> 00:01:32,360
Because there's the best answer key of all, a photo.

17
00:01:32,630 --> 00:01:39,160
A picture of mine derived from someone else's picture is highly likely to become a second version of A, a second version of B.

18
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:42,280
When this gets worse, it becomes what's called plagiarism.

19
00:01:42,281 --> 00:01:50,520
Even if it's not plagiarism per se, there are cases where you don't know better, reference someone else's picture, and follow it, then both you and your picture end up looking shabby.

20
00:01:50,859 --> 00:02:08,380
For example, you think you're referencing for historical accuracy, and you draw based on someone's picture, but it turns out to be something the person made up. Or, there are several pictures with a certain design and pose, and you reference and follow them, but it turns out to be some group's identity. That kind of case.

21
00:02:08,381 --> 00:02:15,260
While designs in photos are also designs composed by someone else, they have less room for variation than pictures.

22
00:02:15,261 --> 00:02:18,860
It's the idea that designs derived from reality are actually often many.

23
00:02:20,350 --> 00:02:27,040
Elements based on reality, like the human body or light, must be drawn using only photo references.

24
00:02:27,041 --> 00:02:34,720
Even subconsciously, I've basically never seen someone who imitated another's style and surpassed the imitated picture or the artist.

25
00:02:34,721 --> 00:02:35,920
In the end, they become a follower.

26
00:02:36,190 --> 00:02:44,800
This might sound like an old-timer's talk, but to not stay in the third tier and at least reach the second tier, it's right to refrain from imitation of pictures.

27
00:02:44,801 --> 00:02:48,281
Also, imitating a design can be a legal issue.

28
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:53,600
Especially when you think someone's picture is cool and copy the whole framework directly.

29
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:58,080
You can be inspired by other people's pictures.

30
00:02:58,081 --> 00:03:05,760
However, if you're going to draw using references, I'd hope those references are always photos.

31
00:03:05,761 --> 00:03:08,240
Enjoy looking at pictures a lot in your daily life.

32
00:03:08,241 --> 00:03:12,320
I often look at clothing malls or fashion show photos.

33
00:03:12,614 --> 00:03:18,860
They're plenty enough to appreciate good clothing silhouettes, body shapes, realistic composition, and color.

34
00:03:18,861 --> 00:03:32,140
These designs too are designs composed by someone else, but if they're not overly adapted forms of clothing, and are familiar designs, and you only reference a few elements, you can filter and use them enough.

35
00:03:32,220 --> 00:03:42,540
For example, reference the color scheme of an accessory and apply it to a top and bottom, or apply the silhouette of European-style clothing to an Eastern-style clothing design.

36
00:03:43,180 --> 00:03:45,420
Just because the picture I'm drawing is fantasy.

37
00:03:46,015 --> 00:03:48,600
There's no need to search for references hard.

38
00:03:48,601 --> 00:03:52,280
In realistic-style drawing, it's good to use things that actually exist.

39
00:03:52,281 --> 00:04:00,600
Something completely original is highly likely to be unfamiliar to the public and feels off-putting, so it's not very effective in commercial art.

40
00:04:00,601 --> 00:04:07,160
At the beginner-intermediate stage, rather than creating something new, focus on packaging familiar things well.

41
00:04:07,161 --> 00:04:15,080
First, at the rough sketch and rough coloring stage, do a rough design on a silhouette we commonly see or are familiar with.

42
00:04:15,510 --> 00:04:25,240
When drawing fantasy armor or futuristic clothes, rough work while thinking of armor that actually existed or modern clothes.

43
00:04:25,320 --> 00:04:32,920
Since you can gradually modify it from there, actively search for and collect real materials.

44
00:04:32,921 --> 00:04:39,960
While gathering references like this, I've gotten inspirations, and though not complete, they've been taking shape in my head.

45
00:04:39,961 --> 00:04:43,521
Drawing is done with the hand, but you should draw with the head.

46
00:04:43,745 --> 00:04:48,060
It's not cool for the hand to go first and the head to think later.

47
00:04:48,061 --> 00:04:53,180
Pro creators have a solid form in their head, and the hand just expresses it.

48
00:04:53,181 --> 00:04:54,940
It's called drawing what's in your head, like printing.

49
00:04:54,941 --> 00:04:57,020
I'm still finding it hard, but I'm working on it.

50
00:05:00,375 --> 00:05:09,040
Likewise, designing and drawing by relying only on the references I've collected, without having built up a form in my head, isn't good either.

51
00:05:09,041 --> 00:05:12,480
That's like having the references up on the monitor next to the picture while you work.

52
00:05:12,775 --> 00:05:14,480
You have to create something.

53
00:05:14,481 --> 00:05:20,000
In this case, the process of using references as a springboard and developing into creative imagination is hard.

54
00:05:20,001 --> 00:05:22,320
Reference use should be limited.

55
00:05:22,375 --> 00:05:30,521
If you draw with the reference up, at first you only consult it a little, but eventually you end up fifty, seventy percent similar.

56
00:05:30,650 --> 00:05:35,500
There's a high chance of it becoming a piece with ambiguous direction, neither yours nor theirs.

57
00:05:35,635 --> 00:05:41,580
That said, you also can't draw it eighty or ninety percent the same so the big framework becomes similar.

58
00:05:41,581 --> 00:05:45,820
Taking the entire framework of something isn't what we call referencing.

59
00:05:46,075 --> 00:05:50,540
Also, putting up several references and consulting them little by little is strange.

60
00:05:50,541 --> 00:05:57,340
I've really seen a lot of rookie concept artists get pushed around by and enslaved to the many references they put up.

61
00:05:58,465 --> 00:06:02,780
As I keep saying, as creators we have to think flexibly.

62
00:06:02,781 --> 00:06:07,900
Design and style have no fixed answer, so you shouldn't put the reference up like an answer key and draw.

63
00:06:07,901 --> 00:06:11,980
Concept art finds freshness in small differences.

64
00:06:11,981 --> 00:06:17,100
The reference you put up will be an element that hinders your new inspiration.

65
00:06:17,101 --> 00:06:21,420
You have to see with your eyes, understand with your head, and convey with your hands.

66
00:06:21,421 --> 00:06:26,140
You must not move it from your eyes to your hand without going through your head.

67
00:06:26,141 --> 00:06:33,820
However, if the picture I'm drawing needs to match historical accuracy or be close to it, then I have no choice but to draw with the reference up.

68
00:06:33,821 --> 00:06:37,820
Honestly, it's not a task that requires creativity, so let's leave it out of the discussion.

69
00:06:37,821 --> 00:06:43,541
Just reference the material for historical accuracy of the design, and put differentiation in the pose or composition.

70
00:06:46,225 --> 00:06:48,730
So now let's gather references.

71
00:06:48,731 --> 00:06:59,450
Based on the brief planning document from last time, using words like coat, cape, polygons, transparency, light, and so on, I've gathered various references.

72
00:06:59,451 --> 00:07:03,850
For references, in my case, I search on Google or Pinterest.

73
00:07:03,851 --> 00:07:11,290
First, for the references needed for the work, I search the keyword on Google, download them to one place, and appreciate them.

74
00:07:11,291 --> 00:07:14,970
Honestly, even photos unrelated to the keyword give me small shocks.

75
00:07:14,971 --> 00:07:17,210
If I like them, I download them.

76
00:07:17,211 --> 00:07:19,210
They also give new inspiration.

77
00:07:19,211 --> 00:07:24,010
Pinterest has overwhelmingly more picture references than photo references.

78
00:07:24,011 --> 00:07:27,690
So I don't look at it much during reference gathering before work.

79
00:07:27,691 --> 00:07:31,290
Instead, I appreciate Pinterest a lot in daily life.

80
00:07:31,291 --> 00:07:35,130
I also appreciate ArtStation a lot in daily life and get inspiration there.

81
00:07:38,640 --> 00:07:43,680
Let me try to take in as much as I can from these photo references I searched on Google.

82
00:07:44,240 --> 00:07:47,360
I appreciate them without skimping on time.

83
00:07:47,361 --> 00:07:50,560
I allocate relatively a lot of time to this.

84
00:07:50,640 --> 00:07:51,640
It's a warm-up.

85
00:07:51,840 --> 00:07:53,680
You have to get yourself fired up.

86
00:07:54,645 --> 00:08:06,080
Compared to when I rush into the work without this time, pressed for time, it's much better for the work itself when I spend enough time appreciating references and organizing my thoughts before starting.

87
00:08:06,640 --> 00:08:09,360
And the satisfaction with the process was better too.

88
00:08:09,361 --> 00:08:13,920
While appreciating references, sometimes I close my eyes and envision the design.

89
00:08:13,921 --> 00:08:21,440
It's great when you think, I want to get this down on the canvas quickly, I want to draw fast.

90
00:08:23,230 --> 00:08:26,800
Once you have a rough concept, start the work.

91
00:08:26,801 --> 00:08:32,000
Do the silhouette-focused sketch and rough coloring from last time.

92
00:08:32,001 --> 00:08:38,960
Once the details are set, even without references, you can draw well enough with just the concept in your head.

93
00:08:38,961 --> 00:08:49,360
At this time, rather than short-term memory, which is recalling the collected references while working, the long-term memories of things you've seen, felt, and been impressed by in life are more helpful.

94
00:08:49,361 --> 00:08:51,760
That's why you should appreciate a lot in daily life.

95
00:08:56,150 --> 00:09:01,400
Once the design is set and the later detailed rendering begins, I take out the references again.

96
00:09:01,401 --> 00:09:03,880
At this time, I put them up next to me and work.

97
00:09:03,881 --> 00:09:05,320
By photo references, I mean.

98
00:09:05,321 --> 00:09:08,920
At this time, the photo references just have the meaning of quality boost.

99
00:09:08,921 --> 00:09:12,920
Reference for material or reference for fine patterns, that's it.

100
00:09:12,921 --> 00:09:16,440
For a medieval fantasy, you can reference medieval patterns.

101
00:09:16,441 --> 00:09:19,320
For an Eastern-style picture, you can reference Eastern patterns.

102
00:09:19,560 --> 00:09:23,601
Photo references for material make the drawing more realistic.

103
00:09:24,285 --> 00:09:31,800
This latter-half reference use for materials is mainly done when doing illustration, and isn't strictly necessary for concept art.

104
00:09:31,801 --> 00:09:38,200
But if you have time to spare, I think you can be greedy for artistic quality.

105
00:09:38,201 --> 00:09:43,240
At this time, even if you use the references for ten minutes, the framework I've set won't shake.

106
00:09:43,241 --> 00:09:45,080
And it must not shake.

107
00:09:45,081 --> 00:09:51,800
In conclusion, from the moment you pick up the pen to the moment the picture ends, you should be able to complete the picture without looking at references.

108
00:09:52,125 --> 00:09:54,920
A flexible mind is your value.

109
00:09:54,921 --> 00:09:56,840
Not the references you've gathered in abundance.

110
00:09:58,480 --> 00:10:00,160
It can be hard at first.

111
00:10:00,161 --> 00:10:02,800
Please practice slowly and steadily.

112
00:10:02,801 --> 00:10:07,280
If you always drew faces with a celebrity's face next to you, try drawing without it.

113
00:10:07,500 --> 00:10:11,120
If you drew the human body with a reference up, try drawing without it.

114
00:10:11,121 --> 00:10:17,840
Slowly look at the body reference, turn it off, draw, then turn it on to compare and check.

115
00:10:17,841 --> 00:10:18,880
It's simple.

116
00:10:18,881 --> 00:10:23,680
If you absolutely must look at a reference, at least turn it off for as long as you're holding the pen.

117
00:10:23,681 --> 00:10:26,480
If you need to look at the reference again, put the pen down and look.

118
00:10:26,785 --> 00:10:38,741
Even if your performance drops for now because you don't look at the reference while drawing, even if it's a hard and lonely job without a reference beside you, I hope you'll keep this up and expect a quick rise in skill.

119
00:10:39,990 --> 00:10:44,840
At the start of the class, I said we'd set off on a journey to draw delicious pictures.

120
00:10:44,841 --> 00:10:53,880
I think there's a difference in value between a cook who just follows a recipe someone else put up on a stand, and a cook who confidently draws out the taste.

121
00:10:54,180 --> 00:10:55,640
You have to become a famous-restaurant chef.

122
00:10:56,179 --> 00:10:59,640
A cook who can't even fry a dumpling without a recipe is just sad.

123
00:10:59,641 --> 00:11:03,640
This time became like a lecture containing my subjective thoughts.

124
00:11:03,641 --> 00:11:09,880
They might be my old-timer thoughts, but these are stories I thought you'd want to ponder at least once.

125
00:11:10,020 --> 00:11:11,060
That's why I did it.

126
00:11:11,061 --> 00:11:12,900
Thank you for listening.

127
00:11:12,901 --> 00:11:17,540
As I say many times, I won't force my thoughts on you.

128
00:11:17,810 --> 00:11:20,660
I hope you'll think actively.

129
00:11:20,661 --> 00:11:25,220
Next time, let's proceed with the sketch, the first step of concept art practice.
